w examples of Hawaii being genuinely contested.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Hawaii remains one of the most Democratic-leaning states in presidential elections, and the party's nominee starts with a clear advantage in this open-seat contest. Neither side has a candidate-specific edge: no incumbent is on the ballot, no general-election polls are available, and candidate fundraising has not yet been reported. A Hawaii Public Radio poll in October found voters want Congress to check President Trump and trust Democrats more on the issues, consistent with the state's lean.\n\nThe expected margin is roughly fifteen points for the Democrat, with wide uncertainty because the evidence is limited to fundamentals. Republicans would need an unusually strong national environment or a nominee with unusual appeal in the islands to make this competitive. The most likely outcome is a comfortable Democratic win, with the main question being the size of the margin rather than the winner.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9253, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8734, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8703, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9295, "margin": 16.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.874, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8784, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.877, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.874, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.8862, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.878, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.8719, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.8743, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.8792, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "20